#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:5
#: ca878d3c09be45cdbb8b62e92049495d
msgid "Yaw Tuning for Tilt Vectored Yaw Configurations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:7
#: 9d5f0ce9d4904503b971aee178a589e9
msgid "Tuning the yaw axis is a bit different if tilt vectored yaw is utilized. Tilt Vectored Tailsitters and Quadplanes like Tilt-Tricopter configurations use tilting of the motors to accomplish yaw will need a slightly different tuning process since the yaw response is not being driven by motor rotation speed differentials. These systems will need the FF term determined and set before tuning D and P terms for yaw. (This information is repeated in the :ref:`Tailsitter tuning <tailsitter-tuning-guide>` page)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:9
#: 21a4be0a2e3041c990008c74d5f614a5
msgid "First, follow Steps 1 - 4 of the normal :ref:`QuadPlane Tuning Process Setup<quadplane-vtol-tuning-process>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:10
#: 842e27e611324d3e9e0982533a8c26b5
msgid "Normally, the vehicle is stable enough with the default PIDs to do a first test hover in QSTABILIZE or QHOVER. Do a short hover flight with logging enabled such that the :ref:`Harmonic Notch Filters<common-imu-notch-filtering>` can be setup. Its important to remove as much motor noise as possible in order to get a good tune using a throttle based harmonic notch filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:11
#: 3e67b422121d45e597a848cc1ebda0c2
msgid "Once the notch filters are set, do another hover flight but briefly do short, but sharp yaw stick movements in both directions. Do not endanger the vehicle, but try to get several short full stick movements right and left. You should be able to hold full yaw stick each direction for a second or so, returning to neutral for a second in-between direction reversals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:12
#: e2eddc3ada894a928bc2b5161298a45f
msgid "Download the :ref:`dataflash log<common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner>` for analysis. In order to determine the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF<Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF>` term, use a log analyzer like https://plot.ardupilot.org and setup the following plots on the same scale, using the \"Add Expression\" button:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:19
#: 6f734ed1a782430b8617d8e7d899fa60
msgid "Next adjust the X value above until the magnitudes of both plots are about equal. This will now be value for the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF<Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF>` term. Set the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_I<Q_A_RAT_YAW_I>` term to equal this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:21
#: e1caa6f77a58401c97f1925eeb38cd68
msgid "Below is an image of this where the X value is adjusted until the two curves are the same amplitude, with a value of x = 0.1. This is then set as the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF<Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF>` and :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_I<Q_A_RAT_YAW_I>` values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:26
#: 2bdd0dd7db884db985811be319131945
msgid "Now you can hover again, and begin increasing the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_D<Q_A_RAT_YAW_D>` term, either iteratively, or using :ref:`common-transmitter-tuning`, until it oscillates and then reduce it to 1/2 to 1/3 that value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tilt-vectored-yaw-tuning.rst:27
#: 745a65f3b78b47debb6acade1a752d51
msgid "Then increase the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_P<Q_A_RAT_YAW_P>` term, until it oscillates and then reduce it  1/2 to 1/3 that value."
msgstr ""
